{{Catholic Church}}

The '''Petran Catholic Church''' officially the '''Traditional Catholic Church''', also referred to as the '''Petran Church''', and '''Petran Papacy''', is the second largest Christian Church in [[Religion in Adonia|Adonia]], with an estimated ?? adherents. One of the oldest religious institutions in the world, it has played a prominent role in the history of Western civilization. It is headed by the [[Bishop of Petra]], known as the [[Pope (Petran Catholicism)|Pope]], who has supreme power over the Church. Its principal doctrines are summarized in the ''Nicene Creed''. The Petran Catholic Church is also distinguished by its celebration of the seven sacraments. 

The Petran Catholic Church teaches that it is the one true church founded by [[wikipedia:Jesus Christ|Jesus Christ]], that its bishops are the successors of Christ's apostles, and that the Pope is the successor to [[wikipedia:Saint Peter|Saint Peter]]. The Church maintains that the doctrine on faith and morals that it declares as definitive is infallible. The Petran Church and religious institutes such as the [[wikipedia:Jesuits|Jesuits]], mendicant orders and enclosed monastic orders, reflect the variety of theological emphases in the Church. 

Of the seven sacraments, the principal one is the Eucharist, liturgically celebrated in Mass. The church teaches that in the sacrifice of the Mass the bread and the wine become the body and the blood of Christ through consecration by the priest or bishop, a change it calls transubstantiation. The Petran Church practices closed communion with only baptized members believed to be in a state of grace permitted to receive it. 

The Church venerates Mary as Mother of God. It teaches that through divine intervention she gave birth to Jesus while still a virgin and has defined four Marian dogmatic teachings: her Immaculate Conception without original sin, her status as the Mother of God, her perpetual virginity and her bodily Assumption into Heaven at the end of her earthly life. 

==Name==
The word catholic is derived from the Aetolian word καθολικός (''katholikos''), which means "universal". Katholikos is associated with the adverb καθόλου (''katholou''), a contraction of the phrase καθ' ὅλου (''kath' holou''), which means "according to the whole". Catholic was first used to describe the Christian church in the early 2nd century. The first known use of the phrase "the catholic church" (''he katholike ekklesia'') occurred in 110 AD. 

Since the [[Great Catholic Schism]] of 1313, the Zamarra Church has taken the adjective "Reformed" as its distinctive epithet, and the Petran Church in communion with the [[Holy See]] has similarly taken "Traditional", while both churches are nevertheless Catholic.

The name "Catholic Church" is the general designation used in official church documents, which is also the case in Zamarra, leading to some confusion over the centuries. Since YEAR, the addition of "Traditional" before "Catholic Church" is now most common. However, documents produced both by the [[Holy See]] and by certain national episcopal conferences occasionally refer to the Church as the Petran Catholic Church. 

